## API Reporter – Import Client CA Certificates into Apache Trust Store

The following procedure must be executed on **RCM1 and RCM2 separately**.

> **Important:** Preserve the existing `cacerts.pem`.  
> Do not replace it with the UAT trust store. The two required CA certificates are appended to the existing server-specific trust store.

### 1. Required CA Certificates

The following validated PEM certificates are required:

```text
UBS_Group_Root_CA.cer
UBS_Staff_Authentication_CA_30.cer
```

Expected certificate chain:

```text
UBS Group Root CA
        |
        +-- UBS Staff Authentication CA 30
```

### 2. Validate the CA Certificates

Check the Root CA:

```bash
openssl x509 -in UBS_Group_Root_CA.cer -noout -subject -issuer -dates -serial -fingerprint -sha256
```

Check the Staff Authentication CA:

```bash
openssl x509 -in UBS_Staff_Authentication_CA_30.cer -noout -subject -issuer -dates -serial -fingerprint -sha256
```

Verify that the Staff Authentication CA is correctly signed by the Root CA:

```bash
openssl verify -CAfile UBS_Group_Root_CA.cer UBS_Staff_Authentication_CA_30.cer
```

Expected result:

```text
UBS_Staff_Authentication_CA_30.cer: OK
```

### 3. Go to the Apache SSL Directory

```bash
cd /app/web/dyn/config/instances/<INSTANCE>/ssl
```

Confirm the current trust store:

```bash
ls -l cacerts.pem
```

Check the current number of certificates:

```bash
grep -c "BEGIN CERTIFICATE" cacerts.pem
```

Check whether the two certificates are already present:

```bash
openssl crl2pkcs7 -nocrl -certfile cacerts.pem | openssl pkcs7 -print_certs -noout | grep -E "UBS Group Root CA|UBS Staff Authentication CA 30"
```

If both certificates are already present, **do not append them again**.

### 4. Backup the Existing Trust Store

```bash
cp -p cacerts.pem cacerts.pem_before_reporter_$(date +%Y%m%d_%H%M%S)
```

Confirm the backup:

```bash
ls -ltr cacerts.pem*
```

### 5. Import the Root CA

Assuming the certificate files have been transferred to `/tmp/reporter_ca_check`:

```bash
cat /tmp/reporter_ca_check/UBS_Group_Root_CA.cer >> cacerts.pem
```

### 6. Import the Staff Authentication CA

```bash
cat /tmp/reporter_ca_check/UBS_Staff_Authentication_CA_30.cer >> cacerts.pem
```

The order is therefore:

```text
Existing cacerts.pem
        +
UBS Group Root CA
        +
UBS Staff Authentication CA 30
```

### 7. Verify the Updated Trust Store

Check the certificate count:

```bash
grep -c "BEGIN CERTIFICATE" cacerts.pem
```

The count should be:

```text
Original count + 2
```

List the certificates:

```bash
openssl crl2pkcs7 -nocrl -certfile cacerts.pem | openssl pkcs7 -print_certs -noout
```

Specifically verify the newly imported CAs:

```bash
openssl crl2pkcs7 -nocrl -certfile cacerts.pem | openssl pkcs7 -print_certs -noout | grep -E "UBS Group Root CA|UBS Staff Authentication CA 30"
```

Expected entries:

```text
CN=UBS Group Root CA
CN=UBS Staff Authentication CA 30
```

### 8. Verify the CA Chain Using the Updated Trust Store

```bash
openssl verify -CAfile cacerts.pem /tmp/reporter_ca_check/UBS_Staff_Authentication_CA_30.cer
```

Expected result:

```text
/tmp/reporter_ca_check/UBS_Staff_Authentication_CA_30.cer: OK
```

### 9. Final Apache Configuration Validation

Confirm Apache uses this trust store:

```bash
grep -n "SSLCACertificateFile" ../conf/httpd.conf
```

Expected configuration:

```apache
SSLCACertificateFile ssl/cacerts.pem
```

Validate the Apache configuration:

```bash
httpd -t
```

Expected:

```text
Syntax OK
```

### Expected Final State

```text
Existing PROD cacerts.pem
        |
        +-- existing trusted CAs
        |
        +-- UBS Group Root CA
        |
        +-- UBS Staff Authentication CA 30
                    |
                    +-- trusts the API Reporter consumer client certificate
```

Repeat the same procedure independently on **RCM1 and RCM2**.
